#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "2230e2dd-e47a-5db3-bb07-f89d0396440b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6573" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0$, use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let\n\n$$\ny_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= ue^{-t} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{-t} - ue^{-t} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= u^{″}e^{-t} - 2u^{′}e^{-t} + ue^{-t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} - 3y^{′} - 4y &= 0 \\\\\n(u^{″}e^{-t} - 2u^{′}e^{-t} + ue^{-t}) - 3(u^{′}e^{-t} - ue^{-t}) - 4ue^{-t} &= 0 \\\\\nu^{″}e^{-t} - 5u^{′}e^{-t} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{-t}(u^{″} - 5u^{′}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^t \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'$. Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u'$, we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = u e^{-t}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left(\\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right)\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0$, we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bd03341b-1190-51fa-b3f0-6fd921e3430c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6574" :question/problem "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?\n\n1. $u''=0$\n2. $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n3. $u''-u=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2}(x) &= u(x)y_{1}(x) \\\\\n&= u(x)e^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{2} &= ue^{3x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{′} &= u^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} \\\\\ny_{2}^{″} &= u^{″}e^{3x} + 6u^{′}e^{3x} + 9ue^{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} - 6y^{′} + 9y &= 0 \\\\\n(u^{″}e^{3x} + 6u^{′}e^{3x} + 9ue^{3x}) - 6(u^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x}) + 9ue^{3x} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{3x}(u^{″} + 6u^{′} + 9u - 6u^{′} - 18u + 9u) &= 0 \\\\\ne^{3x}u^{″} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ne^{3x} \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5fc7fc-2f90-556e-a8ba-841ee4345c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123611} {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4f5584-8fc7-5337-ab56-9e4c5e615f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123613} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3ceaf6-e888-5fb4-82cc-4ead9996c36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123614} {:answer/id #uuid "3a307f38-89b4-5782-9efc-cba840fb9d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5512a1-ab4e-5f23-a650-68685dcdaff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123610}] :question/id #uuid "ed6836b6-963c-5315-9c37-229f40227bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64958" :question/problem "The function $y_{1}(x) = x^{3}$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} - 3y = 0$ for $x > 0$. Use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_{2}(x)$ to the equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e87fa0-cbe5-5c8f-a25f-becfe555c70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} - 2u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123623} {:answer/id #uuid "eae3394f-8ba9-59fe-98b7-7cd80dd4b515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xu^{″} + 5u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123624} {:answer/id #uuid "2d798297-8017-5f5d-8095-f835eb411ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} + 7u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123625} {:answer/id #uuid "33f2244c-0581-5881-9396-fff7fef1171e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} + 3u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123626} {:answer/id #uuid "43008340-987a-5945-b230-c8a5d3713242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} - 5u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d798297-8017-5f5d-8095-f835eb411ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xu^{″} + 7u^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123625} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f9e258c-0697-50bb-854f-78cc38193521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123622}] :question/id #uuid "4a18ec5a-2e1b-5a0c-b567-88ee298d1b67" :question/math-academy-id "q-64995" :question/problem "The function $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation $x^{2}y^{″} + 5xy^{′} - 5y = 0$ for $x > 0$. If $y_{2} = u(x)y_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)$?"}]}
